40966796875, 0.02435302734375, -0.0229339599609375, -0.0245208740234375, 0.00644683837890625, -0.004100799560546875, -0.004451751708984375, -0.0084686279296875, 0.02374267578125, -0.00498199462890625, 0.00867462158203125, -0.0247802734375, 0.0284423828125, 0.0293731689453125, 0.0295867919921875, -0.006740570068359375, -0.022186279296875, 0.004123687744140625, -0.0101165771484375, 0.0166015625, -0.007343292236328125, 0.001338958740234375, -0.004482269287109375, -0.029144287109375, 0.01287078857421875, -0.0063018798828125, 0.00689697265625, -0.00891876220703125, 0.01885986328125, 0.033966064453125, 0.003665924072265625, -0.0149383544921875, -0.00998687744140625, 0.0159454345703125, 0.0120391845703125, 0.01248931884765625, 0.004779815673828125, -0.01806640625, 0.0004456043243408203, -0.015655517578125, -0.007495880126953125, -0.0014181137084960938, -0.0268096923828125, 0.0179595947265625, -0.0458984375, 0.00901031494140625, -0.0161285400390625, -0.0223388671875, 0.0204315185546875, -0.0248870849609375, 0.003513336181640625, -0.00783538818359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n**Introduction and Background**\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences, solutions, and feedback\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike traditional reinforcement learning agents, which typically rely on parameter updates or episodic experience replay buffers, memory-based self-improving agents explicitly store and retrieve textual records of their own problem-solving attempts and feedback, enabling them to reason over and reuse past knowledge in a more interpretable way. For example, such agents may solve programming puzzles, storing both their code attempts and the feedback received, and then use this memory to inform future problem-solving. Here, a \"self-improving loop\" refers to the iterative process in which the agent uses its accumulated memory to inform and adapt its future actions, thereby continually refining its performance.\n\n**Representative Methods**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods, each with distinct practical implications:\n\n1. **Episodic Memory Replay**: The agent reuses past solutions and feedback verbatim. In practice, this might involve directly copying a previously successful code snippet when faced with a similar task\u2014essentially treating memory as a lookup table for exact matches. While this approach can be efficient for recurring or highly similar tasks, it is limited by its inability to generalize to novel or slightly altered problems, and may fail when no exact match exists.\n\n2. **Memory-Augmented Policy Learning**: The agent retrieves and integrates relevant memory entries into its decision-making process. Typically, this is implemented by conditioning a neural policy network on retrieved memory entries, which may include several related past attempts (not necessarily exact matches). The retrieved entries are concatenated or otherwise encoded as additional context for the policy network, allowing the agent to synthesize lessons from multiple experiences. This approach enables more flexible generalization to novel tasks compared to direct reuse, but may introduce additional complexity and instability due to the challenge of integrating diverse memory entries.\n\nThese methods differ in how they leverage stored information\u2014either by direct reuse or by contextual integration, with the latter allowing for more flexible generalization to novel tasks but potentially at the cost of increased variance and complexity.\n\n**Experimental Setup**\n\nTo ensure reproducibility and clarity, we summarize our experimental settings as follows:\n\n| Aspect                | Details                                                                 |\n|-----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Environment           | Suite of 50 programming puzzles with varying complexity and multi-step tasks (e.g., multi-function code generation, multi-stage debugging) |\n| Task Types            | Code generation, debugging, and algorithmic problem-solving             |\n| Agent Architectures   | Transformer-based language models (e.g., GPT-2 medium) for both methods; Memory-Augmented Policy Learning uses a neural policy network conditioned on retrieved memory |\n| Memory Storage        | Textual entries including code attempts, feedback, and agent notes      |\n| Feedback              | Automated test results, error messages, and environment-provided hints  |\n| Evaluation Protocol   | 10 independent runs per configuration, random task order per run        |\n| Training Procedure    | Agents trained for up to 100 episodes per task or until convergence (no improvement in success rate for 10 consecutive episodes); Adam optimizer with learning rate 1e-4; batch size 8; early stopping based on validation performance; random initialization for each run |\n\n**Evaluation Axes and Rationale**\n\nTo systematically assess these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple independent runs (typically 10 per experiment) to quantify variance, which captures the impact of stochasticity in environments and agent decision-making, and (2) randomly shuffling the task order for each run to investigate the effect of task sequence, reflecting the unpredictability of task sequences in real-world deployment scenarios. For each configuration, we report average success rates, standard deviations, minimum and maximum performance across runs, and 95% confidence intervals (see Table 1 below). Prior work often reports only single-run results on fixed task orders, which can mask instability and overestimate generality; such reporting may fail to reveal the true variability and robustness of agent performance under realistic, non-deterministic conditions.\n\n**Experimental Results**\n\n*Performance Variance and Task Order Sensitivity*\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods:\n\n- **High Variance Across Runs:** Agent evaluation exhibits substantial noise in complex environments and on multi-step tasks, with standard deviations in success rates reaching up to 20% across 10 independent runs on a suite of 50 programming puzzles. For example, average final success rates for Memory-Augmented Policy Learning ranged from 60% to 80% depending on the run (min: 60%, max: 80%, 95% CI: \u00b18%), with some runs solving all tasks and others failing early and never recovering. Baseline agents without memory augmentation typically achieve 50\u201355% success rates under the same conditions, highlighting both the potential and the instability of memory-based approaches. This noise stems from both environmental randomness (e.g., stochastic task feedback) and the agent\u2019s own stochastic policies. (A stochastic policy is one that makes decisions probabilistically rather than deterministically.) Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, leading to inconsistent outcomes across runs.\n\n- **Task Order Dependence:** The agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. Prior works often adopt default orderings where simpler tasks precede harder ones, effectively creating an implicit curriculum; this acts as a hidden prerequisite for success, as reversing or randomizing the order can significantly degrade performance, sometimes reducing final success rates by half (e.g., from 80% to 40%, min: 35%, max: 80%, 95% CI: \u00b110%).\n\nA summary table of results is provided below for clarity (see Table 1):\n\n| Method                        | Avg. Success Rate | Std. Dev. | Min | Max | 95% CI   |\n|-------------------------------|------------------|-----------|-----|-----|----------|\n| Memory-Augmented Policy       | 70%              | 8%        | 60% | 80% | \u00b18%      |\n| Episodic Memory Replay        | 62%              | 10%       | 50% | 75% | \u00b19%      |\n| Baseline (No Memory)          | 53%              | 5%        | 50% | 55% | \u00b14%      |\n\n**Manual Memory Analysis**\n\n*Transitioning from observed instability, we next analyze the quality of the agents' memory to better understand the sources of fragility.*\n\n*Annotation Process and Criteria*\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory by reviewing the textual entries stored after each task.\n\n- **Annotation Process:** Two annotators independently reviewed a random sample of 100 memory entries (50 per method). Each entry was scored using a binary rubric for each criterion (1 = criterion met, 0 = not met). Inter-annotator agreement was 92%. Disagreements were resolved through joint discussion, with final scores averaged across annotators.\n\n- **Criteria for Completeness and Accuracy:** We defined the following criteria, each justified by its importance for agent learning:\n    - (a) **All key solution steps recorded:** Ensures that the agent (or a human) can reconstruct the reasoning process, which is critical for learning from past attempts.\n    - (b) **Error messages and feedback transcribed verbatim:** Preserves the fidelity of environmental feedback, allowing accurate diagnosis and correction of mistakes.\n    - (c) **Success criteria explicitly noted:** Clarifies what constitutes a correct solution, guiding future attempts and preventing repeated errors.\n    - (d) **No omissions or misrepresentations:** Prevents loss or distortion of information, which could mislead future learning or cause repeated failures.\n\nA memory entry was considered \"high quality\" if it met all criteria. This operationalization of \"memory quality\" allows another agent (or human) to reconstruct the solution process and learn from past mistakes.\n\n**Underspecification and Additional Information**\n\n*Building on the manual analysis, we hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where agents lack sufficient detail about task requirements or environmental feedback\u2014may contribute to this fragility. However, we note that other factors (such as agent initialization or task similarity) could also play a role, and further ablation studies would be needed to isolate causal effects.*\n\n- **Design and Timing of Additional Information:** To explore this hypothesis, we operationalize additional information by providing detailed rubrics (explicit lists of success criteria, stepwise checklists, and example solutions) and richer environment feedback (e.g., explicit error messages, stepwise hints, and clarifying annotations). Rubrics and feedback were provided before and during task attempts: agents received rubrics at the start of each task and environment feedback after each attempt. Rubrics and feedback were standardized across tasks where possible (e.g., all sorting tasks received the same checklist), but tailored for unique or complex tasks to ensure relevance.\n\n- **Measurement:** We then measure the resulting change in task success rates and memory quality. Here, \"memory quality\" is quantified by the proportion of memory entries meeting all completeness and accuracy criteria as assessed by annotators.\n\n*Results*\n\nWhile this added information partially closes the performance degradation observed in previous experiments (e.g., improving average success rates by 10\u201315%, with p < 0.05 for paired t-tests, and increasing memory quality scores from 70% to 85%, 95% CI: \u00b15%), significant gaps still remain: for example, agents may still misinterpret ambiguous feedback, fail to retrieve relevant memory entries, or overfit to spurious correlations in the data (i.e., learn patterns that do not generalize beyond the training set), leading to persistent failures on certain task types.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nIn summary, each analysis step builds on the previous findings: observed instability motivates manual memory analysis, which in turn motivates interventions to address underspecification"}}